### Which @angular/* package(s) are relevant/related to the feature request?

forms

### Description

In Signal Forms, a field has no way to access its parent field. This makes it hard to implement metadata that is defined once on an ancestor and inherited by all descendants.

My use case is a custom `displayErrorsOn` property (touched | dirty | always | never) that controls when validation errors are shown in the UI. I usually set it once on the root form, but it can be overridden on any nested group or field. With Reactive Forms, each control walked up the tree (`control.parent`) to the nearest ancestor that defined it.

In Signal Forms I can set metadata on the root field, but a child field can't read it because it can't reach its ancestors. The only option is to set the same metadata on every descendant manually, instead of defining it once at the parent level.

### Proposed solution

Expose a reference to the parent field, for example:
`fieldState.parent // ReadonlyFieldTree<unknown> | undefined`

This would let UI components and custom logic walk up the tree and resolve inherited settings.

Alternatively, metadata keys could support inheritance directly, resolving the value from the nearest ancestor that defines it:
`const DISPLAY_ERRORS_ON = createMetadataKey<Mode>({ inherit: true });`

### Alternatives considered

Recursive schema with `applyEach` - apply the metadata to every descendant:

```
export function displayErrorsOn(path: SchemaPath<unknown>, mode: DisplayValidationErrorsOn): void {
    const branch: Schema<unknown> = schema(p => {
        metadata(p, DISPLAY_ERRORS_ON, ctx => ({ mode, target: ctx.fieldTreeOf(path) }));
        applyEach(p, branch);
    });

    apply(path, branch);
}
```
This works, but it relies on `applyEach` with object paths, while the docs describe it only for arrays. So I'm not sure it's safe to depend on long-term.

