body_text:
`HoodieRecordReader` hands back the log records of a file group only as a stream, via `getLogRecordsOnly()`. A caller that needs to look one up by key, or by position, has to drain that iterator and rebuild an index, and in the buffered case that duplicates in memory what the record buffer is already holding.

The older reader does not have this gap. `HoodieMergedLogRecordReader` exposes both halves over the same buffer:

    @Override
    public Iterator<BufferedRecord<T>> iterator() {
      return recordBuffer.getLogRecordIterator();
    }

    public Map<Serializable, BufferedRecord<T>> getRecords() {
      return recordBuffer.getLogRecords();
    }

Code moving from the merged log record reader to the file group reader loses random access.

Proposal: add the indexed accessor to the `HoodieRecordReader` interface, so it is available to the callers that already program to that interface rather than to one concrete reader, with each implementation deciding how to serve it. `HoodieFileGroupReader` can return the record buffer's own map, keyed by record key or by record position depending on the merge mode, at no extra cost. `HoodieLsmFileGroupReader` merges as it streams, so it has no index to hand back and would materialize one, which its javadoc should say so callers can prefer the iterator when a single pass is enough.

Separately, `HoodieFileGroupReader#getLogRecordsOnly()` throws a `NullPointerException` when the file group has no log records to merge, because the record buffer is only built when there is something to merge into the base file. The new accessor should return an empty map in that case, and the existing iterator accessor an empty iterator.

